**Read:** [Systems Engineering Handbook — NASA](../../../engineering/mechanical-engineering-and-machine-design/sources/Systems-Engineering-Handbook-NASA.lingua#r_7ZQFZ9E1NAVG27SE2R5S24PDBQ), chapter 5, especially verification, validation and transition; [Measurement Process Characterization — NIST/SEMATECH](../../../inquiry/metrology/sources/Statistical-Handbook.lingua#r_6S3221KSX1KKGPJNXKC3N022NN), chapter 6, Process or Product Monitoring and Control, progressively through acceptance sampling, charts, time series and the lithography case. Read [Measurement uncertainty in conformity assessment — JCGM 106](../../../inquiry/metrology/sources/GUM-Conformity.lingua#r_0VFKYJZJC8BXM2T2TSY58DD6SY) in full when developing acceptance decisions.

The preceding sections explain how contaminants move, interact and become observations. Now ask what evidence supports a claim about a built system and how that claim is maintained as conditions change. Installation, operating performance, product acceptance and monitoring have related but distinct questions.

Begin with the claim and operating domain. Study sample design and decision risks before interpreting a passing result. Then establish a baseline, examine changes over time and investigate competing explanations. Three studies join qualification, monitoring and acceptance to a useful manufactured object.

Use paper designs, simulations and published observations. Keep the source's industrial scope and any historical standard references explicit. A mathematical sampling plan does not establish a regulated process requirement. Specialist microbial laboratory methods remain deferred in [Clean manufacturing and contamination control — from familiar objects to controlled processes](../Clean-manufacturing-and-contamination-control.lingua#r_7D8SQ8BVQDZQBDBGC6DP0JAMZN).

**Work:** Choose a benign manufactured item, such as a precision optical mount. State what contamination could prevent it doing, what facility evidence would help, and what separate evidence is needed about the item itself.

**Return:** [Clean manufacturing and contamination control — from familiar objects to controlled processes](../Clean-manufacturing-and-contamination-control.lingua#r_7D8SQ8BVQDZQBDBGC6DP0JAMZN).
**Continue in clean manufacturing:** [State the claim before choosing a test](State-the-claim-before-choosing-a-test.lingua#r_7WE13D9TDTC14ESWCKQ4N0SN5J).

